<section xml:id="sec-mod4-worked-examples">
  <title>Worked Examples</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 45 minutes.</p>

    <p>
      Read each example slowly. The goal is not just to see what the
      answer is, but to internalize the proof <em>template</em>.
      <xref ref="ex-mod4-we-1"/> is the basic <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> pattern you will
      reproduce on the assessment. <xref ref="ex-mod4-we-2"/> shows how the Algebra of
      Limits replaces direct <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> work for
      rational expressions. <xref ref="ex-mod4-we-3"/> shows the Monotone Convergence
      Theorem in its most common use: a recursively defined sequence
      whose limit is not visible at a glance.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 1: DIRECT EPSILON-N PROOF
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod4-we-1">
    <title>A Direct <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> Proof</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove directly from the definition that
        <md>a_n = \frac{1}{n^2} \longrightarrow 0.</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Scratch work (this section is part of the solution
        process; always do this before writing the proof).</alert>
        We want to choose, given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, an index
        <m>N</m> such that
        <md>n \geq N \Longrightarrow |a_n - 0| = \frac{1}{n^2} \lt \varepsilon.</md>
        Solving <m>1/n^2 \lt \varepsilon</m> for <m>n</m>: this happens
        exactly when <m>n^2 \gt 1/\varepsilon</m>, i.e.,
        <m>n \gt 1/\sqrt{\varepsilon}</m>. So we should choose
        <m>N</m> to be any natural number exceeding
        <m>1/\sqrt{\varepsilon}</m>. The Archimedean Property
        guarantees such <m>N</m> exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        We could also use the weaker but simpler bound
        <m>1/n^2 \leq 1/n</m>, requiring only <m>n \gt 1/\varepsilon</m>.
        Either choice works. The proof below uses this simpler bound,
        which avoids the square root, and takes <m>N \gt 1/\varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. By the Archimedean Property,
        there exists <m>N \in \N</m> with <m>N \gt 1/\varepsilon</m>.
        Then for every <m>n \geq N</m>,
        <md>
          <mrow>|a_n - 0| = \left|\frac{1}{n^2}\right| = \frac{1}{n^2} \amp\leq \frac{1}{n}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp\leq \frac{1}{N}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp\lt \varepsilon,</mrow>
        </md>
        where the first inequality uses <m>n \geq 1</m> (so <m>n^2 \geq n</m>),
        the second uses <m>n \geq N</m>, and the third uses
        <m>N \gt 1/\varepsilon</m>.
        Since <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> was arbitrary, <m>a_n \to 0</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The proof has a fixed structure you will reproduce many times:
        <em>start the formal proof with <q>Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m></q>,
        produce <m>N</m>, then verify the bound on <m>|a_n - L|</m>
        for <m>n \geq N</m>.</em> The scratch work lives outside that
        formal proof but must be shown; the scratch work is what
        justifies the specific choice of <m>N</m>. The chain of
        inequalities in the proof does the verification. Each step is
        labeled with a reason: you should do this in your own proofs
        too.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 2: ALGEBRA OF LIMITS
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod4-we-2">
    <title>A Rational Limit via the Algebra of Limits</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate
        <md>\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{3n^2 + 2n - 1}{4n^2 + 5}.</md>
        Do not use an <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> argument; use the
        Algebra of Limits (Zorn's Theorem 2.5) directly.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Set-up.</alert>
        The standard technique for a ratio of polynomials in <m>n</m>
        is to divide numerator and denominator by the highest power of
        <m>n</m> appearing in the denominator; here the highest power
        is <m>n^2</m>. This gives the
        denominator a nonzero limit (its leading coefficient), as the
        quotient rule requires, and when the numerator's degree is no
        larger, it forces every term to converge.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Dividing numerator and denominator by <m>n^2</m>, we rewrite
        the general term:
        <md>\frac{3n^2 + 2n - 1}{4n^2 + 5} = \frac{3 + \frac{2}{n} - \frac{1}{n^2}}{4 + \frac{5}{n^2}}.</md>
        We now evaluate the limits of the numerator and denominator
        separately, using the Algebra of Limits. Throughout, we use
        the known limit <m>1/n \to 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Numerator.</em> By the product rule,
        <md>\frac{2}{n} = 2 \cdot \frac{1}{n} \longrightarrow 2 \cdot 0 = 0, \qquad
        \frac{1}{n^2} = \frac{1}{n} \cdot \frac{1}{n} \longrightarrow 0 \cdot 0 = 0.</md>
        The constant sequence 3 has limit 3. Applying the sum rule
        (twice),
        <md>3 + \frac{2}{n} - \frac{1}{n^2} \longrightarrow 3 + 0 - 0 = 3.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Denominator.</em> The constant 4 has limit 4, and
        <m>5/n^2 \to 0</m> by the product rule applied to
        <m>5 \cdot (1/n^2)</m>. By the sum rule,
        <md>4 + \frac{5}{n^2} \longrightarrow 4 + 0 = 4.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Quotient.</em> The limit of the denominator is 4, which is
        nonzero. By the quotient rule,
        <md>\frac{3 + \frac{2}{n} - \frac{1}{n^2}}{4 + \frac{5}{n^2}}
        \longrightarrow \frac{3}{4}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Hence
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{3n^2 + 2n - 1}{4n^2 + 5} = \frac{3}{4}.</m>
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        Once the basic limit <m>1/n \to 0</m> is established (<xref ref="ex-mod4-we-1"/>
        covers <m>1/n^2</m>; <m>1/n</m> is the classical case), every
        rational expression in <m>n</m> reduces to a short sequence of
        limit-law applications. The only subtle point is the nonvanishing
        denominator hypothesis in the quotient rule: you must confirm
        <em>in the limit</em> that the denominator is nonzero, as this
        solution does (the denominator's limit is 4). If the numerator's leading degree had
        exceeded the denominator's, the method would break down (the
        sequence would diverge to infinity); if the degrees were equal,
        the limit is always the ratio of leading coefficients.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 3: MONOTONE CONVERGENCE FOR A RECURSIVE SEQUENCE
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod4-we-3">
    <title>A Recursive Sequence and the Monotone Convergence Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define the sequence <m>(a_n)</m> recursively by
        <md>a_1 = 1, \qquad a_{n+1} = \sqrt{2 + a_n} \quad (n \geq 1).</md>
        Prove that <m>(a_n)</m> converges and find its limit.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        The formula for <m>a_n</m> is not in closed form, so a direct
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> proof is impractical. We instead:
        (1) show <m>(a_n)</m> is increasing and bounded above, (2)
        conclude it converges by the Monotone Convergence Theorem, and
        (3) find the limit by letting <m>n \to \infty</m> in the
        recursion.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 1: The sequence is bounded above by 2.</alert>
        We claim <m>a_n \lt 2</m> for every <m>n \in \N</m>, and
        prove this by induction.
        <em>Base case.</em> <m>a_1 = 1 \lt 2</m>. <em>Inductive step.</em>
        Suppose <m>a_n \lt 2</m>. Then
        <md>a_{n+1} = \sqrt{2 + a_n} \lt \sqrt{2 + 2} = \sqrt{4} = 2,</md>
        using that <m>\sqrt{\cdot}</m> is monotone increasing on
        <m>[0, \infty)</m>. By induction, <m>a_n \lt 2</m> for all <m>n</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 2: The sequence is increasing.</alert>
        We claim <m>a_n \lt a_{n+1}</m> for every <m>n \in \N</m>,
        again by induction. <em>Base case.</em>
        <m>a_1 = 1 \lt \sqrt{3} = a_2</m>. <em>Inductive step.</em>
        Suppose <m>a_n \lt a_{n+1}</m>. Adding 2 and applying the
        (strictly monotone) square root,
        <md>a_{n+1} = \sqrt{2 + a_n} \lt \sqrt{2 + a_{n+1}} = a_{n+2}.</md>
        So <m>a_{n+1} \lt a_{n+2}</m>. By induction, <m>(a_n)</m> is
        strictly increasing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 3: Apply the Monotone Convergence Theorem.</alert>
        By Steps 1 and 2, <m>(a_n)</m> is monotone increasing and
        bounded above (by 2). By the Monotone Convergence Theorem,
        <m>(a_n)</m> converges to some limit <m>L \in \R</m>. All
        terms satisfy <m>1 \leq a_n \lt 2</m> (the lower bound holds since
        <m>a_1 = 1</m> and the sequence is increasing), so the limit
        satisfies
        <m>1 \leq L \leq 2</m> (limits preserve non-strict
        inequalities; see <xref ref="thm-mod4-limit-order"/>).
        The strict bound <m>a_n \lt 2</m> yields only <m>L \leq 2</m>
        in the limit.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 4: Identify the limit.</alert>
        Squaring the recursion <m>a_{n+1} = \sqrt{2 + a_n}</m> gives
        <md>a_{n+1}^2 = 2 + a_n \qquad (n \geq 1).</md>
        The shifted sequence <m>(a_{n+1})</m> also converges to
        <m>L</m>: given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, take the <m>N</m> that
        works for <m>(a_n)</m>; if <m>n \geq N</m>, then
        <m>n + 1 \geq N</m>, so <m>|a_{n+1} - L| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
        Letting <m>n \to \infty</m>, we use the Algebra of Limits
        (Zorn's Theorem 2.5): the left side converges to
        <m>L \cdot L = L^2</m> by the product rule, and the right side
        converges to <m>2 + L</m> by the sum rule. The two sides are
        the same sequence, so by uniqueness of limits
        (<xref ref="thm-mod4-limit-unique"/>),
        <m>L^2 = 2 + L</m>, i.e.,
        <m>L^2 - L - 2 = 0</m>,
        which factors as <m>(L - 2)(L + 1) = 0</m>. So
        <m>L = 2</m> or <m>L = -1</m>. Since <m>L \in [1, 2]</m>,
        we conclude <m>L = 2</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        This is the archetypal pattern for a recursively defined
        sequence. Three moves, always in this order:
        (1) <em>prove boundedness by induction</em>, often guessing
        the bound by first solving the fixed-point equation informally;
        (2) <em>prove monotonicity by induction</em>, often driven by
        a monotone function applied to the recursion; (3) <em>apply
        the Monotone Convergence Theorem (MCT) to get a limit, then solve a fixed-point equation for
        its value.</em> Note how the Completeness Axiom enters through
        MCT: in an ordered field without completeness, such as
        <m>\Q</m>, a bounded monotone sequence need not converge. For
        example, the decimal truncations
        <m>1, 1.4, 1.41, 1.414, \ldots</m> of <m>\sqrt{2}</m> are
        rational, each is at least the one before, and all are less
        than 2. In <m>\R</m> they converge to <m>\sqrt{2}</m>: the
        <m>k</m>th truncation is within <m>10^{1-k}</m> of
        <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, and <m>10^{1-k} \to 0</m> (since
        <m>10^{k-1} \geq k</m>, so <m>10^{1-k} \leq 1/k</m>). They have no limit
        in <m>\Q</m>. A limit in <m>\Q</m> would also be a limit in
        <m>\R</m>, because every positive real <m>\varepsilon</m> has
        a positive rational below it; uniqueness of limits would then
        force that limit to be <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, which is irrational.
        Step 4 squares the recursion so that only the product and sum
        rules are needed. Letting <m>n \to \infty</m> directly in
        <m>a_{n+1} = \sqrt{2 + a_n}</m> would require the
        <em>continuity</em> of the square root, which you will study
        in <xref ref="ch-mod6"/>. Zorn
        (&#xa7;2.3) takes the direct route, using continuity of the
        square root informally.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
